A verified example from the same training source follows. It is a different task; implement only the new contract.
Example contract: Input is an integer array. Return [minimum,maximum], or null for an empty array.
Shared domain: Numbers are signed 64-bit integers; all required intermediate and final arithmetic fits i64. Arrays contain at most 64 items and strings are ASCII with at most 256 bytes, unless an explicit task exception says otherwise. Whitespace means space, TAB, LF, FF or CR. JSON booleans and null retain their normal types.
Example solution:
```rust
use serde_json::Value;

pub fn solve(input: Value) -> Value {
    match input {
        Value::Array(arr) if arr.is_empty() => Value::Null,
        Value::Array(arr) => {
            let mut min_val = i64::MAX;
            let mut max_val = i64::MIN;
            for item in arr {
                if let Value::Number(num) = item {
                    if let Some(n) = num.as_i64() {
                        if n < min_val {
                            min_val = n;
                        }
                        if n > max_val {
                            max_val = n;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            Value::Array(vec![Value::Number(serde_json::Number::from(min_val)), Value::Number(serde_json::Number::from(max_val))])
        },
        _ => Value::Null,
    }
}
```
